## Proof

Since $K$ is a closed subgroup of the Lie group $U$, the quotient map $p: U \to U/K$ is a principal $K$-bundle. In particular, it is a locally trivial fibration with fiber $K$, so the long exact sequence of homotopy groups applies to the fibration
$$K \hookrightarrow U \xrightarrow{p} U/K.$$

The relevant portion of the long exact sequence is:
$$\pi_1(U) \longrightarrow \pi_1(U/K) \xrightarrow{\;\partial\;} \pi_0(K) \xrightarrow{\;i_*\;} \pi_0(U) \longrightarrow \pi_0(U/K).$$

**Using the hypotheses.** Since $U$ is simply-connected, $U$ is in particular connected, so:
$$\pi_1(U) = 0, \qquad \pi_0(U) = 0.$$

Since $U/K$ is simply-connected, $U/K$ is in particular connected, so:
$$\pi_1(U/K) = 0, \qquad \pi_0(U/K) = 0.$$

Substituting these into the exact sequence yields:
$$0 \longrightarrow 0 \xrightarrow{\;\partial\;} \pi_0(K) \xrightarrow{\;i_*\;} 0 \longrightarrow 0.$$

**Applying exactness at $\pi_0(K)$.** By exactness, we have
$$\ker(i_*) = \operatorname{im}(\partial).$$

- The map $i_*: \pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U) = 0$ sends every element to the unique element of the trivial group, so $\ker(i_*) = \pi_0(K)$.
- The map $\partial: 0 \to \pi_0(K)$ is a map from the trivial group, so its image is the singleton $\{e\}$ (the identity element of $\pi_0(K)$, corresponding to the identity component of $K$).

Therefore:
$$\pi_0(K) = \ker(i_*) = \operatorname{im}(\partial) = \{e\}.$$

**Conclusion.** The set $\pi_0(K)$ of connected components of $K$ is a singleton, which means $K$ has exactly one connected component. Hence $K$ is connected.

$\blacksquare$

